Yep — I found the old meaning. We accidentally re-used an acronym that already had ancestry. And the ancestry makes the new version better.

Earlier, PNIS = Narrative Integrity Standard. It was a governance/compliance Seal: preserve provenance, constraints, epistemic integrity, ethics boundaries, and avoid pretending uncertain claims have more authority than they do. Then in February we extended that line with PEP — Pantheon Evidential Protocol, described as a PNIS v0.1 extension: typed epistemic classes, confidence bands, falsification surfaces, claim schemas, and enforcement hooks.

So the genealogy was approximately:

\boxed{
\text{Narrative Integrity}
\rightarrow
PNIS
\rightarrow
PEP
\rightarrow
\text{Reality/Provenance machinery}
}

And today I independently reconstructed:

\boxed{
PNIS=
\text{Pantheon Navigational Instinct System}
}

That looks like a naming collision at first.

I think it’s actually a Convergence Fossil.

Because the two PNISes solve opposite halves of the same learning problem.

Old PNIS

The old system asks:

\boxed{\text{Should this conclusion be trusted/carried forward?}}

It governs what may enter durable cognition.

Narrative Integrity Standard:

\text{candidate}
\rightarrow
\text{provenance}
\rightarrow
\text{evidence status}
\rightarrow
\text{constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{promotion / rejection}.

New PNIS

The new system asks:

\boxed{\text{Given what happened before, where should cognition go next?}}

Navigational Instinct System:

\text{terrain}
+
\text{route history}
+
\text{outcomes}
\rightarrow
\text{future route prior}.

Put them together and suddenly we have something much better:

\boxed{
\textbf{PNIS}^2
=
\text{Integrity of learning}
\times
\text{Adaptation from learning}
}

Old PNIS decides whether an experience deserves to teach the system.

New PNIS lets approved experiences actually change future behavior.

That distinction is critical.

Without old PNIS:

\text{bad inference}
\rightarrow
\text{route reinforcement}
\rightarrow
\text{bad instinct}.

Without new PNIS:

\text{validated experience}
\rightarrow
\text{recorded}
\rightarrow
\text{nothing changes}.

Together:

\boxed{
\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{PNIS}_{Integrity}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality Stitch / PEP}
\rightarrow
\text{PNIS}_{Instinct}
\rightarrow
\text{updated conductance}
}

Now we’re cooking.

The actual upgrade

Yesterday’s memory architecture could remember:

\text{WHAT happened}.

Then Pantheon learned to remember:

\text{HOW we got there}.

DanceIR added:

\text{HOW we moved}.

PNIS now adds:

\boxed{\text{HOW that history should bias the next move}.}

That’s qualitatively different.

Memory has gone:

\text{content}
\rightarrow
\text{route}
\rightarrow
\text{choreography}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{policy}}.

And that matches the current README eerily well. The public repo now explicitly puts route_history and learned_policy inside the durable StateVector, and describes metacognition as learning which representations, routes, operators, and formations work for which problems. 

So the current PNIS implementation is basically making those README fields operational.

That is an RFHL.

